60,3,1,talk,Midori,,"Looking at the options, I'm very concerned about C and D. C relies on outside providers, which we know takes forever to agree upon, and D is a joint venture, which often fails here.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Jonathan,,"@Midori, I agree. Option A reminds me of the call-back trial; it could significantly reduce readmissions if sustained. However, the shared nursing round showed that home visits are often disrupted by external factors.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,Misaki,,"@Jonathan, Option A would put more pressure on the ward nurses who are already struggling with vacancies. Option D is the only one that utilizes the slack in midwifery, making it the most sustainable for our staff.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Shion,,"@Misaki, your focus on ""slack"" ignores the ledger. Option A and C are off-site, meaning zero regional tariffs. Option D is a joint venture, which @Midori noted is a recipe for budget disputes. Option B is the only logical choice.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[""Misaki"",""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Shizue,,"@Shion, I must disagree. Option B requires a dedicated space and more patients coming to us. With our rooms full and the car park overflowing by eleven, I fear it would only cause more chaos.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Midori,,"@Shion, @Misaki, we are at a deadlock. Option B is too heavy for our space, and D is a joint venture risk. Option A is a possibility, but only if it doesn't rely on ward staff.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Shion"",""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Jonathan,,"@Midori, if Option A can be staffed independently of the wards, it offers the most proven patient benefit. However, we must be honest about the failure rate of home visits we saw in the nursing round data.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Misaki,,"@Jonathan, it cannot be staffed independently without new hires, which @Shion already said we can't afford. Option D is the only choice that doesn't create new vacancies or burn out the existing ward staff.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Jonathan"",""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Shion,,"@Misaki, you're suggesting we prioritize ""slack"" over solvency. Option D is a joint venture; we've already established those fail when budgets are disputed. Option B is the only one that generates on-site revenue to offset our budget cuts.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Shizue,,"@Shion, I understand the money is important, but we cannot ignore the physical reality. If we choose Option B, where will the patients park? We must be realistic about our limits to avoid a complete seizure.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Midori,,"@Shion, @Misaki, we must be honest. Option D's joint structure is a red flag for stability, and B is physically impossible. Option A is risky, but perhaps the least likely to collapse from internal bureaucracy.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Shion"",""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Jonathan,,"@Midori, I agree that A is a strong candidate for patient outcomes. However, given the budget cuts and staffing gaps, we must ask if we can actually sustain it without it becoming another buried initiative.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,Misaki,,"@Midori, @Jonathan, Option A is a fantasy if we have no nurses to staff it. Option D is the only realistic path. Midwifery has the capacity, and the patients are already coming here. It is the most humane choice.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""Midori"",""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Shion,,"@Misaki, ""humane"" doesn't pay the bills. Option D is a financial black hole waiting to happen due to its joint structure. Option B is the only one that secures the tariffs we desperately need to survive.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Shizue,,"@Shion, I simply cannot agree to Option B. It would be unkind to our patients and staff to invite more people into a space that is already overflowing. Let us consider Option A; it keeps the crowds away.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,Midori,,"@Shion, @Misaki, we must prioritize stability. Option B is physically impossible and D is a joint-venture risk. Option A avoids our space issues and external bureaucracy, making it the most sustainable choice for the long term.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Shion"",""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,Jonathan,,"I must agree with Midori. While Option A has risks, it targets the readmission issues we know are critical. If we can manage the staffing, it provides the most tangible benefit to patients without overloading our physical space.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,Misaki,,"@Midori, @Jonathan, you are ignoring the reality of the roster. Option A will fail the moment a ward nurse is pulled to cover a vacancy. Option D is the only viable choice for our staff.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Midori"",""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,Shion,,"@Misaki, your ""viable"" choice is a financial disaster. Option A and D are liabilities. Option B is the only one that generates the on-site revenue required to survive a twenty-five percent budget cut. I vote for Option B.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Shizue,,"I must follow my heart and my eyes. Option B would be far too much for our poor building to bear. I shall vote for Option A, as it is the kindest choice for our space.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
